From 7e709a286a4937c16ed59f78d18196317b344f30 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Reed <reedswork@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 18:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] client: TLS 1.2 RSA key transport, for the TLS_RSA_WITH_*
 suites

Chrome's ClientHello (chrome_hello) offers TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256,
TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384, TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA and
TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA, and servers that prefer them pick them
(pro.ip-api.com picks TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA from Chrome's list).
rustls has no static RSA key exchange, so the handshake failed with
SelectedUnofferedCipherSuite.

KeyExchangeAlgorithm gets an RSA variant, client side only. A suite with
it expects no ServerKeyExchange: after the Certificate (and a stapled
CertificateStatus, if any) comes a CertificateRequest or the
ServerHelloDone. Once the config's verifier has verified the chain, the
client makes a 48-byte pre-master secret (the ClientHello's version, TLS
1.2, then 46 random bytes from the provider), has the config's new
`rsa_key_transport` (a `RsaKeyTransport`, supplied by the application)
encrypt it to the end entity's key, sends it as the ClientKeyExchange's
EncryptedPreMasterSecret and derives the master secret from it, with the
extended master secret when the server agreed to it. Nothing is signed:
the server proves it holds the key by decrypting the secret, without which
its Finished does not verify. Whether the ServerKeyExchange signature is
checked is decided by the suite's key exchange, and a ServerKeyExchange
that does not match the suite is an error, so no suite that signs can skip
the check. The pre-master secret lives on the heap, so moving it copies
nothing, and is zeroized when dropped, and the key block is zeroized once
the record keys are made.

A config with no `rsa_key_transport` fails such a handshake as before. The
server side never selects an RSA suite (ServerKeyExchangeParams::new
treats an RSA group as unreachable). A provider may list RSA suites with
no key exchange group for them. make_cipher_pair's comment says how a CBC
suite's Tls12AeadAlgorithm receives its MAC and cipher keys from the key
block as it is cut today.
